Z||000000||8Ball||08-07-2001||09:49 PM||||Well,<BR>Tomorrow I am going to the junkyard to look at this 87 gt that the owner says is in great shape. He bought it today from an auction and drove it to the junkyard. The headlight motors don't work and it needs a tie rod. Other than that he says it is in great shape and wants 800 bucks for it.<P>If I look at it and it is in good enuf shape I am going to try to borrow the money to buy it, plus a lil extra to get it on the road.<BR>Hopefully My good buddy Glen who has loaned me money on countless other times will come thru for me again. Must be nice to own 75 rentals and 3 buisnesses  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I knew I should have went in with him on that first house.<P>But everyone wish me luck. I just hope this car is as good as the guy says it is. I also hope I don't go out there only to find he knows nothing about Fiero's and it is actually a coupe and an 84 at that. No offense to anyone, I like the coupes, Heck Ilike ALL Fiero's for that matter but this guy has my hopes up.<P>Randy,<P>Hopefully soon to join the ranks of owner once again.||||reg||1
Z||000001||PBJ||08-07-2001||10:50 PM||||best of luck.  The only gt I ever found in the wrecks they wanted $2200.00 for it and the only thing wrong with it was <BR>"after the owner jumped it the rear harness caught the car on fire, just burnt the back end a bit"  <BR>needles to say I did not go and check it out.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/petesigsm.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||08-07-2001||10:54 PM||||Yes, good luck, 8Ball!  Like you say, you never know for sure what you're getting into until you go and look!<P>One time I answered an ad in the Pennypower (local free ad. pub. here in Wichita) that said 1988 Fiero for sale.  So I got the address and looked. It was a 4-cylinder, 5-speed but what I wanted was the 1988 suspension.  I test drove the car, didn't notice anything particularly different from my other Fieros.  Then came back to talk to the guy some more, and looked under the hood.  That's when I saw a <B>socket</B> being used as the slave cylinder push rod!  I had never seen an '88 suspension in the flesh, but I looked underneath the car- didn't notice anything strikingly different from my other Fieros.  Something just wasn't "quite right" about the whole deal, and I was about to decide the "greatly improved 1988 suspension" wasn't anything to write home about.<P>That's when I noticed the VIN number said the car was a 1987!  The guy said, "I'm not sure what year it is," and fetched the title from the house.  "Yeah, it's an '87."  I said, "Sorry, I was looking for an '88 specifically."  He asked, "What did the ad say?"  I showed him it said "1988 Fiero."<P>So the ad was in error.  Oh, well, no sale.         <P>Contrast that to the time I saw an ad for a "plain" 1987 Fiero.  And a low price to match a plain-Jane, 4-cylinder Fiero.  Nothing about V6 or anything. Just "new clutch and transmission."  I called, got the address, went to look and found out it was a GT!<P>Hope your luck turns out more like the 2nd story than the 1st  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Good luck, 8Ball  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  ||||reg||6
